* Intro

The central function of the MBAG project is a quantification of the influence which agricultural activities exert on adjacent areas.
This question intrinsically references the phenomenon of spatial coupling, i.e. the non-independence of points in space.
Trivially, the degree of dependence is intimately linked to the spatial distance between locations:
an observation or measurement is more likely to resemble other measurements in its immediate vicinity, than those further away [@Miller2004].

This is summarized in "Tobler's Law", named after Waldo Tobler who popularly phrased and thereby famously formalized this distance-difference relation [@Tobler1970].
It raises at least two corollaries for MBAA, which we would like to address in this chapter:
1) at what distance would two measurements be practically identical (*"indifference range"*, justifying re-use of an existing measurement installation for MNE);
2) what is the maximum range of common influence factors (*"dependence range"*, i.e. above what distance will observations be mostly independent).

Methods to answer these questions (most importantly variogram modeling) are logistically and computationally demanding.
Obvious reasons are
+ the continuous but anisotropic effects of many common influence factors (e.g. water flow),
+ the overlap of spatial scales of geographic and biological characteristics (climate regions, habitat types, microhabitat),
+ and temporal variability (seasonal to daily periodicities).

These factors cause substantial sample size fragmentation,
which turns out to be prohibitive for a direct analysis of water /chemistry/ parameters (available via the WATINA database) to quantify eutrophication (our initial environmental pressure of interest).
On the other hand, water /levels/ are monitored quasi continuously with data loggers.
Water levels are of relevance as one of the (subsequent) focus modules of MNE.
We also assume that water levels and their analysis can serve as a proxy for spatial coupling of other groundwater related parameters, because they provide hints of the (direct and indirect) interconnectivity of groundwater systems.


In this chapter, we present the outcome of a variogram-based analysis to quantify spatial non-independence (i.e. spatial coupling or spatial co-variation) of groundwater levels.
By applying a slightly modified procedure, tailored to the specificities of the data set, 
we provide general answers to the two questions above for the overall region of Flanders.
We herein summarize the main results; interested readers are referred to 
our much more extensive analysis notes [TODO publish] and
a tutorial on variogram analysis [@VgramTutorial2025].


* Results and Discussion
Spatial coupling arises from common influences and the similarity of environmental factors which determine a given indicator on two locations.
One particularly strong influence was that of soil class (roughly categorized herein, we focus on soil classes "heavy", "light", and "peat").


Peat soil is characteristic for particularly wet habitats, which reflects in a quick run-up to plateau values in the difference-distance-plot ([Fig 1], green curve).
Water levels are all close to the surface, and water level differences of more than 0.25 m are generally rare (note that the figure shows temporally and spatially binned averages).
Consequently, we see evidence of spatial coupling only in a narrow range
(dependence range of `{r} bootstrap_results["peat", "sigma"]`),
which is indicative of the peculiarity of water levels in these habitats.


Heavy soil is characterized by dense particle arrangement.
Water adheres to these particles and is comparatively immobile; fluctuations in water levels are generally expected to be less severe.
We observe a moderate dependence range
(`{r} bootstrap_results["heavy", "sigma"]`), and find that two measurement locations at `{r} threshold["heavy", "threshold"]` distance (indifference range) are expected to differ by less than $0.01 m$ ([Fig 1], dark brown curve).


Compared to that, water is expected to be relatively more mobile on light soil.
This is consistent with the dependence ranges we observe in the difference-distance analysis (`{r} bootstrap_results["light", "sigma"]`).
Differences of more than $0.01 m$ only emerge from `{r} threshold["heavy", "threshold"]` m distance.


These results are rather general, but robust, due to the amount of data and the methodologically required averaging and binning.
Their generality can be misleading.
It is hightly likely that, under typical circumstances, two specific groundwater observation wells would measure highly correlated water levels and water chemistry over a long distance (e.g. if both are located along a river or stream), or that two rather proximal wells are completely independent (e.g. geographic discontinuity, such as a road).
We only included simultaneous (same-day) measurements, and our analysis is agnostic to time-delayed co-variation.
Water flow, in particular, is a major factor.
And as a consequence of flow, we observed that elevation and slope play an important role on variograms; so important that we decided to filter data points for the present analysis to include only well locations with a slope of less than $1 \%$ in between them.
Finally, proximity of surface water bodies or groundwater reservoirs will be carefully considered when evaluating re-use of an existing field installation for MNE.


The system we analyze "top-down" remains complex in its hydrodynamic mechanistics.
What remains despite all the confounding effects is a realistic estimate of the range of inter-dependence of groundwater systems, either through direct connection, or through common externalities.
Our analysis highlights important factors to consider (e.g. soilclass, elevation, temporal effects) and quantified critical distances which could serve as a first reference for policy decisions.



